Copyright (c) 2026 Gary. All rights reserved.

vobuda is free of charge and its source code is not published. This licence
covers the program as it is handed out — the builds on vobuda.com — and it is
written to be read: what you may do, what you may not, and what to do if you
need more.

The source is closed because the program pays for itself another way: by
carrying word of the copyright holder's other products. Nothing else is asked.
Keep that part of the interface, and the program is yours to use for as long
as this licence stands. By installing or using the program you accept these
terms; if you do not, do not install it.

"You" means the person or organisation using the program. An organisation may
accept this licence for its staff; then each of them may do what section 1
allows, and the organisation answers for section 2.


1. WHAT YOU MAY DO

You may, free of charge and for any purpose including your work and your
business:

  a. install and use the program;
  b. run it on as many machines as you like, yours or your organisation's,
     and install it on those machines from a copy kept inside the
     organisation for that purpose;
  c. configure it without limit — every setting is a file in your own folder,
     and what you write in those files is yours;
  d. write themes, looks, monitor skins, button sets, key maps, translations,
     agent descriptions and other configuration files for it — including ones
     that start from the files the program ships with — and share those with
     anyone under any terms you choose, saying that they are for vobuda;
  e. tell the copyright holder what is wrong with it and what you wish it did.


2. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

  a. You may not distribute the program to anyone outside your organisation,
     in whole or in part, as it came or changed in any way — not the
     installer, not the application, not a copy of either. Point them at
     vobuda.com instead; it is free for them too.

  b. You may not sell the program, sublicense it, rent or lend it, or let
     anyone outside your organisation use it — on your machines, over a
     network, or as a hosted or managed service.

  c. The program has a place in its interface that tells you about the
     copyright holder's other products. You may not remove, hide, cover,
     shrink, block or alter it — by changing the program, by a theme or look,
     by a network rule, or by any other means. A setting the program itself
     offers for it is the one exception. This is the price of the program;
     sections 1(c) and 1(d) do not override it.

  d. You may not decompile, disassemble or reverse-engineer the program, or
     otherwise try to recover its source, except where the law that applies
     to you allows it regardless of this clause — for instance to make the
     program work with other software. If that is what you need, ask first
     (section 8); the information is usually easier to get by asking than by
     decompiling.

  e. You may not change the program itself, or make anything derived from it.
     The files section 1 leaves to you — settings, themes, looks, skins,
     button sets, key maps, translations, agent descriptions — are not the
     program and are not covered by this rule.

  f. You may not use the name "vobuda", the copyright holder's other product
     names, or their logos as the name or brand of anything you distribute or
     publish, or in a way that suggests the copyright holder made or endorsed
     it. Saying that a theme or a skin is for vobuda is fine.

  g. You may not remove or alter this licence, the copyright notice, or any
     notice of authorship carried by the program.


3. THE SOURCE CODE

The source code is not published and is not licensed to you: this licence
covers the builds only. If any source reaches you — a leak, an extract, a
file left somewhere it should not have been — you have no right to keep, use,
copy, change or pass it on; it remains the copyright holder's confidential
property. Delete it, and if you can, say where it came from.

People the copyright holder has given access to the repository may read and
change the source for the purpose of sending changes back, on the terms in
CONTRIBUTING.md, and for nothing else.


4. WHAT THE PROGRAM IS BUILT ON

The program contains libraries other people wrote and gave away under their
own terms. Their names, versions, copyright lines and licence texts ship inside
the program — the application menu has "What vobuda is built on", which opens
the file NOTICES.md — and those terms, not this licence, govern those parts.


5. WHAT THE PROGRAM SENDS

The program checks vobuda.com for updates; that check carries no more than the
program's version and the system it runs on. It sends nothing else about you
unless you ask it to: a record of the program's own faults goes out only after
you have turned that on, and never carries your work. What you do with the
agents, tools and shells you run through the program is yours to answer for.


6. IF THIS LICENCE IS BROKEN

If you break section 2, your rights under section 1 end at once. They come
back on their own if you put the breach right within 30 days of the copyright
holder telling you about it in writing, unless the copyright holder has
written in the meantime that this is not the first time. Once your rights have
ended you must stop using the program and delete your copies. Sections 3 and
7 to 9 survive.


7. NO WARRANTY

The program is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or
implied, including but not limited to the warranties of merchantability,
fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement. To the fullest extent
the law allows, the copyright holder is not liable for any loss or damage
arising from the program or its use — including lost data, lost profits and
any indirect or consequential loss — whether in contract, tort or otherwise.
Nothing in this licence excludes or limits liability that the law of your
country does not allow to be excluded, and some laws give you rights that
cannot be taken away by a licence; nothing here takes them.


8. IF YOU WANT MORE

Any of the above can be granted to you in writing — redistribution, a build of
your own, a licence for a product you sell. Ask. The answer is often yes.


